# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  logossession.la — LogosSession, the session manager (Citrinitas
#  layer 11): login with a Γ-seal, screen lock, suspend/resume, and the
#  isolation of one sovereign from another.
#
#  Codex: "Manages login (Γ-seal authentication), session state, screen
#  lock, suspend/resume, multi-sovereign isolation"; "LogosSession manages
#  itself (the session manager's session is the managed session)."
#  OS_LAYERS_DESIGN.md ("logossession.la") is the contract this module
#  implements; where it leaves a choice open, the choice is stated below.
#
#  ── WHAT IT GIVES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  SESSION_KIT(…P…)(…effects…)(crypt)(sec)(fs)(random)
#     = la s. s(adduser)(login)(lock)(unlock)(suspend)(resume)(state)(logout)
#  P, the pure builtins, in this order:
#      str_at ord str_len str_to_int int_to_str chr concat str_eq
#      add sub mul div mod lt int_eq bor bxor error
#  then the effects, in this order:
#      stat read_file open write close rename unlink mkdir chmod
#      clock_gettime
#  `crypt` is the crypt record (crypt_ref.la's contract; CRYPT_FAST,
#  logoscrypt.la, in practice: see PERFORMANCE), `sec` a SEC_KIT record
#  (logossec.la), `fs` an FS_KIT record (aletheiafs.la) built with the same
#  crypt record, `random(n)` the VM's random builtin (passed in, so that a
#  test can make it fail).
#  PBKDF2_KIT(…)(crypt) = pbkdf2 is exported too: the stretch, on its own,
#  for its known-answer gate (SESSION_KIT builds its own from it).
#
#  ── USERS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  A user is the file <root>/users/<name>, mode 0600 in a directory of mode
#  0700, holding exactly
#      logos-user 1\nname=<name>\nsalt=<32 hex>\niter=<n>\nverifier=<64 hex>\n
#  salt = random(16), stretch = pbkdf2(pass)(salt)(iter)(32), verifier =
#  hmac(stretch)("logos/verifier"): never the passphrase, never a key.
#  adduser writes iter = 1000 (ITER, chosen so that a login on the VM takes
#  one to two seconds, see PERFORMANCE); login uses the count the record
#  holds, which must be 1..10^7 (MAXITER: a larger one would hang a login).
#  A name is 1-32 bytes of [a-z0-9_-], so it is one path segment (never . or
#  ..) and a valid logossec token id; a passphrase is any non-empty bytes.
#  The user's AletheiaFS store is <root>/home/<name>.
#
#  ── THE KEYS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Everything derives from the Γ-seal, as the contract fixes:
#      K_U   = hkdf(salt)(stretch)("logos/gamma")(32)
#      K_M   = hkdf("")(K_U)("aletheia/master")(32)   the store's master key
#      K_cap = hkdf("")(K_U)("gamma/root")(32)        the capability root
#      root token = mint(K_cap)(name)(nil)
#  The root token has no caveats: it grants everything K_cap governs, which
#  is exactly this user's own resources (another user's root is another
#  key). Its holder narrows it with logossec's attenuate to delegate.
#
#  ── THE OPERATIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Results are la err. la ok. …; a session is an opaque value.
#      adduser(root)(name)(pass)  ok(name). Creates root, root/users and
#          root/home (mode 0700) when they are missing, and writes the
#          record atomically: <record>.tmp is created afresh (O_EXCL, so a
#          leftover is never opened or reused), made 0600 before a byte is
#          written, then renamed. Refuses, writing nothing: a bad root, a
#          bad name, an empty passphrase, a user that exists, a home that
#          exists (a store sealed under someone else's K_M would never open),
#          a leftover .tmp, a failing random.
#      login(root)(name)(pass)    ok(session): the clock is read, the record
#          checked, the passphrase stretched and the verifier compared
#          (every byte, no early exit), the keys derived, the store opened
#          and probed (a find, so a store that does not open under K_M is an
#          error now, not at the first read), and the session manager's
#          record of the session made: a fresh 16-byte session id, the
#          user, the login time, the number of suspends (0).
#      lock(session)              the locked session (a value, as the
#          contract says; locking a locked session gives it back).
#      unlock(locked)(pass)       ok(the session again): the keys derived
#          again from the passphrase and the user record as it is now, the
#          store reopened, the same record of the session.
#      suspend(session)(state), resume(root)(name)(pass): not built yet;
#          each returns an err. The next step adds them.
#      state(session)             ok(la k. k(name)(store)(token)(guard)(record))
#          for an open session, err for a locked one. The contract names
#          this operation and leaves it open; it is the session's view for
#          its holder: the user's name, the AletheiaFS handle on the user's
#          store (opened under K_M with the guard below), the root token,
#          the guard itself, and the session manager's record of the session
#          as text ("session=<32 hex>\nuser=<name>\nsince=<unix seconds>\n
#          suspends=<n>\n"). K_U itself is not handed out: the session keeps
#          it, and the view carries what derives from it.
#      logout(session)            ok(name): the store's ephemeral session is
#          closed (so every ephemeral object it made can no longer be
#          opened) and nothing of the session is kept.
#  Errors, each prefixed with the operation ("login: …"): "bad root", "bad
#  name", "empty passphrase", "the user exists", "<home> already exists",
#  "cannot create <path>", "write failed: <path>", "random failed", "no
#  clock", "wrong user or passphrase" (a missing user and a wrong
#  passphrase look alike), "bad user record", "home: <AletheiaFS error>",
#  "the session is locked", "the session is not locked".
#
#  ── THE GUARD ADAPTER ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#  The contract opens AletheiaFS with "logossec's verify, partially
#  applied" as its guard, but FS_KIT asks guard(tok)(res)(op) for a Church
#  boolean, while verify takes now last and returns ok/err. vguard (below)
#  is where they meet: vguard(K_cap) = la tok. la res. la op. the realtime
#  clock read (an unreadable clock: FALSE), then
#      verify(K_cap)(nil)(tok)(res)(op)(now)(la m. FALSE)(la id. TRUE)
#  so only ok grants, and every refusal verify can give is FALSE. The
#  revocation list is empty (see HONEST SCOPE).
#
#  ── WHAT A SESSION HOLDS, AND LOCK ──────────────────────────────────
#  An open session holds the name, the root, K_U, the store handle (which
#  holds K_M and the index key), the root token, the guard (which holds
#  K_cap) and the record. A locked one holds only the name, the root and
#  the record: no key and nothing derived from one. Every value that holds
#  a key, and every value an operation hands back, is built by its own
#  constructor (mkopen, mklocked, mkrec, mkview, mkpair, mkuser, vguard),
#  bound when the kit is built and before any secret exists, because on
#  the VM a closure keeps its whole environment alive: a value written
#  inline inside an operation would keep that operation's passphrase,
#  stretch and keys reachable for as long as the value lives (aletheiafs.la's
#  spair and mkeph are the same pattern). The passphrase and the stretch
#  are held by nothing once an operation returns.
#
#  ── PERFORMANCE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Kit style (WM_DESIGN.md, "How fast the VM is"): every builtin and every
#  integer constant is a parameter, the functions are LET-bound once when
#  the kit is built, booleans are applied to thunks; after startup no name
#  reaches the VM's glyph table from this module. The cost is PBKDF2: each
#  iteration is one crypt hmac (the record has no precomputed-key HMAC, so
#  the key schedule is redone every time) and a XOR over eight 32-bit words.
#  MEASURED on the VM with CRYPT_FAST, timed inside the gate's driver (a
#  4-core machine, load about 1): PBKDF2 of 1000 iterations 1.35-1.9 s;
#  adduser 1.7 s, login 1.35 s, unlock 1.44 s; lock, state, logout 4-6 ms;
#  a guard question 7 ms. With CRYPT_REF an HMAC takes about a minute on
#  the VM, so a login would take most of a day: CRYPT_REF meets the
#  contract but is not usable here.
#
#  ── HONEST SCOPE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  1000 iterations is what the VM affords for a login of a second or two;
#  current advice for PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 is hundreds of thousands, so a
#  stolen record (salt and verifier) is far cheaper to attack offline than
#  that advice intends. The count lives in the record, so a faster crypt
#  record can raise it for new users without breaking old ones.
#  The user record is not authenticated (no key exists before login):
#  whoever can write <root>/users can replace a record with one for a
#  passphrase they know and log in, but the K_U they get is not the user's,
#  so they cannot open the user's objects (the home probe fails).
#  A missing user answers at once and a wrong passphrase after the stretch:
#  the two messages are the same, the times are not.
#  One adduser per name at a time: two at once can both find the name free.
#  No revocation: the root token, and every token attenuated from it, stays
#  valid for as long as the user's passphrase and salt do (K_cap is derived
#  from them); bound a delegated token with until=. Revocation needs a
#  persistent revocation list, which logossec leaves to its caller.
#  Every login and unlock opens the store anew, and AletheiaFS keeps each
#  open store's ephemeral-session pipe (two descriptors) for the life of
#  the process, so a process that logs in or unlocks many times runs out
#  of descriptors (about 500 times at the usual limit of 1024).
#  The VM cannot erase memory: a dropped key stays in the heap until the
#  collector reuses its space, and "drops the keys" means unreachable, not
#  overwritten.
#  Engines: the VM only (stat, open, rename, mkdir, chmod, clock_gettime,
#  random, and AletheiaFS itself). PBKDF2_KIT is pure Lingua Adamica, but
#  the host is far too slow for any useful iteration count.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

export SESSION_KIT PBKDF2_KIT

glyph Z   = la f. (la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))(la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))
glyph LET = la v. la k. k(v)

# ── PBKDF2_KIT: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (RFC 8018 5.2) over crypt's hmac ─────
# PBKDF2_KIT(…P…)(crypt) = pbkdf2, pbkdf2(pass)(salt)(iter)(dklen) -> dklen
# raw bytes: block i is U1 ^ … ^ Uiter, U1 = hmac(pass)(salt ++ INT(i)),
# Uj = hmac(pass)(U(j-1)), INT(i) the 4-byte big-endian block number. The
# XOR runs over eight 32-bit words, built by mk8 (a constructor of its own:
# an accumulator built inline would keep every earlier one alive). iter must
# be at least 1 and dklen 1..8160, else it halts loudly.
glyph PBKDF2_KIT = la str_at. la ord. la str_to_int. la int_to_str. la chr. la concat.
    la add. la sub. la mul. la div. la mod. la lt. la int_eq. la bxor. la error. la crypt.
  crypt(la sha256. la hmac. la hkdf. la seal. la aopen. la hex.
  (la C0. la C1. la C2. la C3. la C4. la C8. la C12. la C16. la C20. la C24. la C28. la C31. la C32.
   la C256. la C65536. la C16777216. la C8160.
  LET(Z)(la fix.
  LET(la c. str_to_int(ord(c)))(la b.
  LET(mul(C256))(la u.
  LET(la n. chr(int_to_str(n)))(la B8.
  LET(la a. la b. la c. la d. la e. la f. la g. la h. la k. k(a)(b)(c)(d)(e)(f)(g)(h))(la mk8.
  # the big-endian 32-bit word at byte o of the string that sa reads
  LET(la sa. la o. add(u(add(u(add(u(b(sa(o))))(b(sa(add(o)(C1))))))(b(sa(add(o)(C2))))))(b(sa(add(o)(C3)))))(la rbe.
  LET(la s. (la sa. mk8(rbe(sa)(C0))(rbe(sa)(C4))(rbe(sa)(C8))(rbe(sa)(C12))
                       (rbe(sa)(C16))(rbe(sa)(C20))(rbe(sa)(C24))(rbe(sa)(C28)))(str_at(s)))(la words.
  LET(la A. la W. A(la a0. la a1. la a2. la a3. la a4. la a5. la a6. la a7.
      W(la b0. la b1. la b2. la b3. la b4. la b5. la b6. la b7.
        mk8(bxor(a0)(b0))(bxor(a1)(b1))(bxor(a2)(b2))(bxor(a3)(b3))
           (bxor(a4)(b4))(bxor(a5)(b5))(bxor(a6)(b6))(bxor(a7)(b7)))))(la xor8.
  LET(la w. concat(concat(B8(div(w)(C16777216)))(B8(mod(div(w)(C65536))(C256))))
                  (concat(B8(mod(div(w)(C256))(C256)))(B8(mod(w)(C256)))))(la wbe.
  LET(la A. A(la a0. la a1. la a2. la a3. la a4. la a5. la a6. la a7.
      concat(concat(concat(wbe(a0))(wbe(a1)))(concat(wbe(a2))(wbe(a3))))
            (concat(concat(wbe(a4))(wbe(a5)))(concat(wbe(a6))(wbe(a7))))))(la out.
  # block(prf)(salt)(iter)(i): T_i, 32 bytes; the loop is a tail call
  LET(la prf. la salt. la c. la i. (la u1.
      out(fix(la go. la j. la uj. la A.
          lt(j)(c)(la _. (la un. go(add(j)(C1))(un)(xor8(A)(words(un))))(prf(uj)))(la _. A)(C0))
        (C1)(u1)(words(u1))))
    (prf(concat(salt)(wbe(i)))))(la block.
  # the first n bytes of s
  LET(la s. la n. fix(la go. la i. lt(i)(n)(la _. concat(str_at(s)(i))(go(add(i)(C1))))(la _. "")(C0))(C0))(la take.
  LET(la pass. la salt. la c. la L.
      lt(c)(C1)(la _. error("logossession: pbkdf2: iter must be at least 1"))(la _.
      lt(L)(C1)(la _. error("logossession: pbkdf2: dklen must be 1..8160"))(la _.
      lt(C8160)(L)(la _. error("logossession: pbkdf2: dklen must be 1..8160"))(la _.
        (la prf. (la n.
            take(fix(la go. la i. lt(n)(i)(la _. "")(la _. concat(block(prf)(salt)(c)(i))(go(add(i)(C1))))(C0))(C1))(L))
          (div(add(L)(C31))(C32)))
        (hmac(pass)))(C0))(C0))(C0))(la pbkdf2.
    pbkdf2
  ))))))))))))))
  (0)(1)(2)(3)(4)(8)(12)(16)(20)(24)(28)(31)(32)(256)(65536)(16777216)(8160))

# ── SES_BASE: the pure helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────
# SES_BASE(…P…)(k) hands k, in this order:
#   T F nil cons OK ERR BIND DO len slice find isnat hexok nameok rootok
#   cteq fromhex fld C0 C1 C16 C32
# Results are la err. la ok. …; BIND(r)(f) is f(v) on ok(v) and r's error
# otherwise; DO(e)(k) evaluates e, then k(e). Positions are native ints; a
# string is scanned by index with str_at, and slices are joined by halves.
glyph SES_BASE = la str_at. la ord. la str_len. la str_to_int. la int_to_str. la chr. la concat. la str_eq.
    la add. la sub. la mul. la div. la lt. la int_eq. la bor. la bxor.
  (la C0. la C1. la C2. la C16. la C18. la C32. la C45. la C48. la C57. la C87. la C95. la C97. la C102. la C122.
  LET(la t. la f. t)(la T.
  LET(la t. la f. f)(la F.
  LET(la n. la c. n(n))(la nil.
  LET(la h. la t. la n. la c. c(h)(t))(la cons.
  LET(la v. la e. la o. o(v))(la OK.
  LET(la m. la e. la o. e(m))(la ERR.
  LET(la r. la f. r(ERR)(f))(la BIND.
  LET(la a. la f. f(a))(la DO.
  LET(la s. str_to_int(str_len(s)))(la len.
  LET(la c. str_to_int(ord(c)))(la ordi.
  # slice(s)(i)(j): bytes i..j-1, joined by halves
  LET(Z(la slice. la s. la i. la j.
      lt(add(i)(C1))(j)
        (la _. (la m. concat(slice(s)(i)(m))(slice(s)(m)(j)))(add(i)(div(sub(j)(i))(C2))))
        (la _. lt(i)(j)(la _. str_at(s)(i))(la _. "")(C0))(C0)))(la slice.
  # find(s)(n)(c)(i): the first index >= i holding the byte c, or n (= len s)
  LET(Z(la find. la s. la n. la c. la i.
      lt(i)(n)(la _. str_eq(str_at(s)(i))(c)(la _. i)(la _. find(s)(n)(c)(add(i)(C1)))(C0))
        (la _. n)(C0)))(la find.
  LET(Z(la go. la p. la s. la n. la i.
      lt(i)(n)(la _. p(str_at(s)(i))(la _. go(p)(s)(n)(add(i)(C1)))(la _. F)(C0))(la _. T)(C0)))(la allc0.
  LET(la p. la s. allc0(p)(s)(len(s))(C0))(la allc.
  LET(la o. la lo. la hi. lt(o)(lo)(F)(lt(hi)(o)(F)(T)))(la inr.
  LET(la c. inr(ordi(c))(C48)(C57))(la digit.
  LET(la c. (la o. inr(o)(C48)(C57)(T)(inr(o)(C97)(C102)))(ordi(c)))(la hexc.
  LET(la c. (la o. inr(o)(C48)(C57)(T)(inr(o)(C97)(C122)(T)(int_eq(o)(C95)(T)(int_eq(o)(C45)))))(ordi(c)))(la namec.
  # isnat(s): a canonical decimal of 1..18 digits (no sign, no leading zero)
  LET(la s. (la n. lt(C0)(n)
        (la _. lt(C18)(n)(la _. F)
            (la _. allc(digit)(s)(la _. str_eq(int_to_str(str_to_int(s)))(s))(la _. F)(C0))(C0))
        (la _. F)(C0))(len(s)))(la isnat.
  # hexok(s)(n): s is exactly n lowercase hex digits
  LET(la s. la n. int_eq(len(s))(n)(la _. allc(hexc)(s))(la _. F)(C0))(la hexok.
  # a user name: 1-32 bytes of [a-z0-9_-] (a path segment that cannot be . or ..)
  LET(la s. (la n. lt(C0)(n)(la _. lt(C32)(n)(la _. F)(la _. allc(namec)(s))(C0))(la _. F)(C0))(len(s)))(la nameok.
  LET(chr("0"))(la NUL.
  # a root: non-empty, with no NUL byte (every syscall would see it cut short)
  LET(la s. (la n. lt(C0)(n)(la _. int_eq(find(s)(n)(NUL)(C0))(n))(la _. F)(C0))(len(s)))(la rootok.
  # cteq(a)(b): equal-length strings compared with every byte's difference
  # OR-ed in, so there is no early exit; a length mismatch is public and fails
  LET(Z(la xacc. la a. la b. la n. la i. la acc.
      lt(i)(n)(la _. xacc(a)(b)(n)(add(i)(C1))(bor(acc)(bxor(ordi(str_at(a)(i)))(ordi(str_at(b)(i))))))
        (la _. acc)(C0)))(la xacc.
  LET(la a. la b. (la n. int_eq(n)(len(b))(la _. int_eq(xacc(a)(b)(n)(C0)(C0))(C0))(la _. F)(C0))(len(a)))(la cteq.
  # fromhex(h): the bytes a lowercase hex string (checked by hexok) denotes
  LET(la c. (la o. lt(C57)(o)(la _. sub(o)(C87))(la _. sub(o)(C48))(C0))(ordi(c)))(la hexval.
  LET(Z(la fh. la h. la i. la j.
      lt(add(i)(C1))(j)
        (la _. (la m. concat(fh(h)(i)(m))(fh(h)(m)(j)))(add(i)(div(sub(j)(i))(C2))))
        (la _. lt(i)(j)
                 (la _. (la p. chr(int_to_str(add(mul(hexval(str_at(h)(p)))(C16))(hexval(str_at(h)(add(p)(C1)))))))(add(i)(i)))
                 (la _. "")(C0))(C0)))(la fh.
  LET(la h. fh(h)(C0)(div(len(h))(C2)))(la fromhex.
  # fld(s)(n)(bad)(key)(pos)(k): key at pos, then a value up to a newline:
  # k(value)(the position after the newline); bad otherwise
  LET(la s. la n. la bad. la key. la pos. la k. (la e. (la j.
        str_eq(slice(s)(pos)(e))(key)
          (la _. lt(j)(n)(la _. k(slice(s)(e)(j))(add(j)(C1)))(la _. bad)(C0))
          (la _. bad)(C0))
      (find(s)(n)("\n")(e)))(add(pos)(len(key))))(la fld.
    la k. k(T)(F)(nil)(cons)(OK)(ERR)(BIND)(DO)(len)(slice)(find)(isnat)(hexok)(nameok)(rootok)(cteq)(fromhex)(fld)
          (C0)(C1)(C16)(C32)
  ))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
  (0)(1)(2)(16)(18)(32)(45)(48)(57)(87)(95)(97)(102)(122)

# ── SESSION_KIT: the session manager ───────────────────────────────────
glyph SESSION_KIT = la str_at. la ord. la str_len. la str_to_int. la int_to_str. la chr. la concat. la str_eq.
    la add. la sub. la mul. la div. la mod. la lt. la int_eq. la bor. la bxor. la error.
    la stat. la read_file. la open. la write. la close. la rename. la unlink. la mkdir. la chmod. la clock_gettime.
    la crypt. la sec. la fs. la random.
  SES_BASE(str_at)(ord)(str_len)(str_to_int)(int_to_str)(chr)(concat)(str_eq)(add)(sub)(mul)(div)(lt)(int_eq)(bor)(bxor)
  (la T. la F. la nil. la cons. la OK. la ERR. la BIND. la DO. la len. la slice. la find. la isnat. la hexok. la nameok.
   la rootok. la cteq. la fromhex. la fld. la C0. la C1. la C16. la C32.
  crypt(la sha256. la hmac. la hkdf. la seal. la aopen. la hex.
  sec(la mint. la attenuate. la verify. la within. la confine.
  fs(la fopen. la fput. la fget. la fupdate. la fhistory. la fversion. la ffind. la fverify. la fremove. la fephemeral. la fclose.
  (la ITER. la MAXITER. la C4. la C8. la C13. la C64. la C4096.
  LET(PBKDF2_KIT(str_at)(ord)(str_to_int)(int_to_str)(chr)(concat)(add)(sub)(mul)(div)(mod)(lt)(int_eq)(bxor)(error)(crypt))(la pbkdf2.
  # ── the constructors. Every value that holds a key, and every value an
  # operation hands back, is built by one of these, bound here, when the
  # kit is built and before any secret exists. On the VM a closure keeps
  # its whole environment alive: a record written inline inside an
  # operation would keep that operation's passphrase, stretch and keys
  # reachable for as long as the record lives.
  # An open session: o(name)(root)(K_U)(home store)(root token)(guard)(record)
  LET(la name. la root. la ku. la h. la tok. la g. la rec. la o. la l. o(name)(root)(ku)(h)(tok)(g)(rec))(la mkopen.
  # A locked session: l(name)(root)(record); no key and nothing derived from one
  LET(la name. la root. la rec. la o. la l. l(name)(root)(rec))(la mklocked.
  # The session manager's record of a session
  LET(la sid. la user. la since. la susp. la k. k(sid)(user)(since)(susp))(la mkrec.
  LET(la name. la h. la tok. la g. la rec. la k. k(name)(h)(tok)(g)(rec))(la mkview.
  LET(la a. la b. la k. k(a)(b))(la mkpair.
  LET(la salt. la iter. la ver. la k. k(salt)(iter)(ver))(la mkuser.
  # now(_): ok(unix seconds) from the realtime clock; err when it cannot be read
  LET(la _. (la t. (la sp. (la s. isnat(s)(la _. OK(str_to_int(s)))(la _. ERR("no clock"))(C0))(slice(t)(C0)(sp)))
        (find(t)(len(t))(" ")(C0)))(clock_gettime("0")))(la now.
  # ── THE GUARD ADAPTER. vguard(K_cap) is the guard the user's AletheiaFS
